alsa: take the time64 timespec fix; cpal from master, rodio from a fork

On Debian trixie, 32-bit libasound writes a 16-byte `struct timespec`.
`libc::timespec` stays 8 bytes on arm-unknown-linux-gnueabihf, so
`Status::get_htstamp` writes 8 bytes past the slot its caller reserved,
overwrites the saved lr and returns into address 0. cpal asks for that
timestamp on every callback, so it takes the first note played.

The fix spans three crates:

  alsa-sys 0.6.1     diwic/alsa-sys#20, released 2026-07-31 — compiles a
                     probe measuring sizeof(snd_htimestamp_t) against the
                     ALSA headers and, when it is 16, defines alsa-sys' own
                     16-byte timespec instead of re-exporting libc's.
  alsa 0.12.1        diwic/alsa-rs#158, released 2026-07-31 — `alsa::timespec`
                     (alsa-sys') replaces libc::timespec in pcm, rawmidi
                     and ump.
  cpal master        RustAudio/cpal#1285, squash-merged as f040088 — bumps
                     alsa to 0.12.1 and alsa-sys to 0.6.1 and carries
                     timestamps as `alsa::timespec`. No 0.18.x release will
                     carry it: 0.18.2 (2026-08-16) still asks for alsa ^0.11,
                     and master is already versioned 0.19.0.

So the two alsa crates are plain version requirements (the patch releases —
alsa 0.12.0 still takes its timespec from libc, alsa-sys 0.6.0 still
re-exports it), and cpal is pinned to a master rev.

An earlier revision of this patch pinned the head of the then-open #1285.
That rev is now unreachable from every ref in the cpal repo — the PR was
force-pushed and then squash-merged, so none of its branch commits landed on
master — which left the pin one GitHub gc away from breaking every arch at
once. A master rev cannot disappear.

rodio is the reason a fork enters the picture. Released rodio 0.22.2 and
rodio master both require cpal ^0.18, which a 0.19.0 cpal cannot satisfy,
and cargo's fallback for an unmatched [patch] would resolve rodio's cpal
from crates.io — a second cpal/alsa-sys pair, which `links = "alsa"`
forbids. So rodio comes from b0bbywan/rodio branch `cpal-0.19`: rodio
master (880aaba) plus the one-line `cpal = "0.19"` bump upstream will make
itself when cpal 0.19 releases. It still declares version 0.22.2, so qbz's
`rodio = "0.22"` requirement is unchanged.

Across the 0.18 → 0.19 bump the only cpal API change rodio trips is a
rename (`StreamTrait::play` deprecated in favour of `start` — two warnings,
old name still works); qbz names neither cpal nor its types. rodio master
raises MSRV to rustc 1.95, which the builders' rustup-installed stable
satisfies. Revs rather than branches, so a rebuild resolves the same
sources.

Verified with `cargo tree --locked -p qbzd -e normal`: a single alsa-sys
0.6.1 (crates.io) in the graph, alsa 0.12.1 (crates.io), cpal 0.19.0 (git
master), rodio 0.22.2 (git fork), and `cargo check --locked -p qbzd`
passes on amd64. cpal's PulseAudio backend stays in the graph via rodio's
default features; the `pulseaudio` crate is pure Rust (no build script, no
libpulse), so the NEEDED list is unchanged.

Drop this patch once cpal 0.19 is released and rodio requires it; the
fork branch and the alsa version requirements die with it.

